<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfred Deakin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfred Deakin was an Australian lawyer and politician from Victoria, a leader of the federation movement and the second prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. He served three terms between 1903 and 1910 and shaped the early Commonwealth through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protective-tariff">protective tariffs</a>, the arbitration system and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-australia-policy">White Australia policy</a>. Earlier, as a Victorian minister, he had pioneered irrigation and factory legislation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfred-deakin.jpg" alt="Alfred Deakin" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Library of Australia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Alfred_Deakin_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alfred Deakin was born on 3 August 1856 in Fitzroy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>, the younger of two children of William and Sarah Deakin, who were of English and Welsh descent. They had emigrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1850 during an economic depression and moved from Adelaide to Melbourne because of the Victorian gold rush. His father worked in the carrying and coaching trade and later for Cobb and Co, earning enough to keep the family comfortably middle class. Deakin worked as a barrister and journalist and in February 1879, aged 23, stood for West Bourke in the Victorian Legislative Assembly, winning in a general election in July 1880 after a first result was voided.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Deakin held Victorian ministerial office from 1883 and in the 1890s became a leading figure in the federation of the Australian colonies. He was a delegate to the federal conventions, served on the committees that drafted the constitution, campaigned at the referendums and lobbied the British government for its adoption. After Federation in 1901 he was the first attorney-general under his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-barton">Edmund Barton</a>, and he became prime minister on 24 September 1903 when Barton left for the High Court.</p><p>He served three terms, 1903 to 1904, 1905 to 1908 and 1909 to 1910, leading a Liberal Protectionist party that held the middle ground between Free Traders and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australian-labor-party">Australian Labor Party</a>. His second term, the longest, saw laws such as the Copyright Act 1905 and the Quarantine Act 1908 and the founding of the Commonwealth Bureau of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">Census</a> and Statistics and Bureau of Meteorology. In 1909 he joined the Free Traders and Joseph Cook in the Fusion, forming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s first majority government; some, including Sir William Lyne, called it a betrayal. Labor won both houses in 1910, and Deakin led the opposition, campaigning against constitutional amendments in 1911, until he retired in 1913.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1914 he chaired a royal commission on wartime food supply and in 1915 led the Australian delegation to the Panama-Pacific International Exposition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>. A degenerative neurological condition led to his death on 7 October 1919, aged 63. Contemporaries called him Affable Alfred. He is regarded as the principal architect of the Australian settlement, which combined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-australia-policy">White Australia policy</a>, compulsory arbitration, protectionism, state paternalism and support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. He refused a knighthood, appointment to the Privy Council and most honorary degrees.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alfred Deakin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Victorian politicians such as <b>Alfred Deakin</b> led the movement for federation, and Victoria became a state of the Commonwealth of Australia in 1901.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-victoria">Colony of Victoria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-37.81,144.96&amp;year=1896" aria-label="Melbourne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alfred Deakin"><image href="/maps/globes/g150s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4660 5674h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4660 5674h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-37.81,144.96&amp;year=1896">What was here in 1896? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
